comments.body:
@simpnu That neutrality principle is the problem I guess. I was under the impression that the goal was to flag and score apps based on their privacy efforts against trackers. Now all the efforts that are done to run self-hosted analytics etc (like indeed Matomo or Countly, forgot the name of the 3rd one) are efforts that cannot be scored. (Maybe "Network signature" could be used for that?)

We thought about running without these analytics but that's not really an option as it would give us a handicap compared to the competition. That is why we are hosting them ourselves.

I guess we should just accept the fact that although we are a privacy-centric app (which is our main focus btw), even with all the efforts we are still getting flagged with 2 trackers. (Countly and for some reason, Google Firebase Analytics). We do not use Firebase Analytics but we provide a way to do OAuth to a Google API so somehow it gets added by default. That is also the reason why we removed "Login with Facebook" yesterday as it was reporting 4 trackers (Analytics, Login, Places and Share). That Google API functionality however, is something we cannot remove.
